{# The line table on a sell-side document's VIEW screen: the header row, the product rows, the
   main-line charge rows beside them, and the empty row when there is nothing to list.

   Three copies existed — the order's Products table, the invoice's Lines table and the quote's
   Products table — of the same ten columns in the same order, reading the same accessors off
   three line classes that all extend the same base. Unlike the FORM side, nothing here posts and
   no cell carries a field name, which is exactly why the row IS shareable here and was not there.

   The columns are built from `showBatch` rather than passed in, because the three tables differ
   by that ONE column and nothing else. The header, the empty row's colspan and any foot template
   all count the same list, so a table cannot end up one cell out of alignment — which is the
   defect a shared row invites and the one that shows up as a wonky table rather than as an error.

   What is NOT here, and why:

     The <section>, its heading and its padding. The order and the quote head this "Products" with
     8px of top margin; the invoice heads it "Lines" with 20px, its own header padding and its own
     heading type scale. That is four presentational parameters to rebuild a wrapper, which is
     drift with extra steps. Each screen keeps its own chrome and includes the table into it.

     The totals. The invoice carries them as a <tfoot> of this very table while the order and the
     quote carry a box beside it, so the foot arrives as `footTemplate` — a path, not a pile of
     parameters — and is handed the column count it needs for its colspans.

   Parameters
   ----------
   lines            required  the document's lines, in sortOrder. perLineTax/perLineTaxLabel are
                              POSITIONAL and the service that builds them reads the lines in the
                              same order — the collections are mapped OrderBy(sortOrder, id) — so
                              loop.index0 addresses the same row in both.
   perLineTax       required  per-line tax amounts, positional. Pass [] for none.
   perLineTaxLabel  required  per-line tax code labels, positional. Pass [] for none.
   emptyMessage     required  what the empty row says. Each document names itself.
   tableStyle       required  the <table> element's inline style. The invoice collapses its
                              borders because it carries a foot; the order and the quote do not.
   feeRows          optional  the MAIN-LINE charge rows to list beside the products, already
                              filtered by the caller and each {label, amount, quantity}. Shipping
                              rows are charges too but are named in the totals rather than listed
                              as products, so every caller leaves them out. Defaults to [].
   showBatch        optional  true to carry a Batch column — the lot actually shipped, which is
                              what a recall or a warranty claim is traced by. The order and the
                              invoice do; a quote has no lot to name because nothing has been
                              picked. Defaults to false.
   allowUnpriced    optional  true to print TBD instead of $0.00 wherever a line's price, subtotal
                              or tax is null (#255). The quote passes it: shipping and price are
                              figures a quote may legitimately leave unstated, and a stated $0.00
                              would tell the customer it is free. An order and an invoice are
                              priced by definition. Defaults to false.
   showBackorderBadge optional  true to note under the product's name that the line promises units
                              it does not have (#548). Only the order can: an invoice bills what
                              shipped and a quote has ordered nothing. Every line of every SKU
                              nobody has opted in is Fulfilled, so even there this usually renders
                              nothing and the cell reads as it always did. Defaults to false.
   showFeeQuantity  optional  true to print a charge row's quantity in the Quantity column when it
                              is not 1. Only the invoice bills a FRACTION of a charge — "0.4 of
                              the freight" — since #539 stage 5; an order's or a quote's charge
                              row is always the whole charge, so they dash it. Defaults to false.
   nameCellClass    optional  the first cell's class. 'order-product-name-cell' on the order and
                              the invoice, where the CSS wraps a long product name instead of
                              truncating it. Defaults to '' (no class attribute at all).
   cellIdPrefix     optional  when given, the quantity and price cells carry
                              `<prefix>-qty-<line id>` and `<prefix>-price-<line id>`. Only the
                              order has them, and they are addressed by name elsewhere. Defaults
                              to '' (no id attribute).
   footTemplate     optional  a <tfoot> to render inside this table, as a template path. Only the
                              invoice has one — the order and the quote carry a totals BOX beside
                              the table instead — and it is a path rather than parameters because
                              it is a dozen conditional money rows, not a value.
   footContext      optional  what `footTemplate` is rendered with. `columns` is added to it
                              automatically, holding how many columns the header just emitted, so
                              the foot's colspans count the same list and cannot drift from it. #}

{% set batch = showBatch|default(false) %}
{% set unpriced = allowUnpriced|default(false) %}
{% set idPrefix = cellIdPrefix|default('') %}
{% set nameClass = nameCellClass|default('') %}
{% set columns = [
    {label: 'Name'},
    {label: 'U/M'},
    {label: 'SKU'},
    {label: 'Weight'},
    {label: 'Quantity', align: 'center'},
    {label: 'Price', align: 'right'},
    {label: 'Subtotal', align: 'right'},
    {label: 'Tax Code'},
    {label: 'Tax $', align: 'right'},
]|merge(batch ? [{label: 'Batch'}] : []) %}

<div class="wide-table-wrap">
    <table class="user-manager-table order-products-table" style="{{ tableStyle }}">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                {% for column in columns %}
                    <th{% if column.align is defined %} style="text-align:{{ column.align }};"{% endif %}>{{ column.label }}</th>
                {% endfor %}
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for line in lines %}
                <tr>
                    <td{% if nameClass %} class="{{ nameClass }}"{% endif %}>
                        <strong style="color:var(--admin-blue-hover);">{{ line.name }}</strong>
                        {% if showBackorderBadge|default(false) and line.backorderedUnits > 0 %}
                            <br><small class="order-line-backorder-badge">{{ line.fulfillmentStatus }}: {{ line.backorderedUnits }} of {{ line.quantity|qty }} awaiting stock{% if line.restockEta %}, ETA {{ line.restockEta|date('Y-m-d') }}{% endif %}</small>
                        {% endif %}
                    </td>
                    {# The conventional line (#601, #659): the U/M is the term the line was
                       written against, the quantity is counted in it, and the price is per it.
                       A line entered in base units reads exactly as it always did. #}
                    <td>{{ line.displayUnitLabel }}</td>
                    <td>{{ line.sku|default('-') }}</td>
                    <td>{{ line.weight|default('-') }}</td>
                    {# `|qty` is the application's one quantity rule, and it lives in
                       App\Service\DisplayNumber rather than in whichever template remembered it:
                       up to four decimals — the scale `NUMERIC(14,4)` actually holds — and none at
                       all when the figure is whole, so 2.5 reads "2.5" and 3 reads "3".
                       `number_format(0)` is what the rule is NOT: it rounded a line of 2.5 cases
                       to 3 while the column went on holding 2.5. Money is `|amount`, a unit price
                       is `|price`. #}
                    <td style="text-align:center;"{% if idPrefix %} id="{{ idPrefix }}-qty-{{ line.id }}"{% endif %}>
                        {{ line.quantityEntered|qty }}
                        {% if line.unitOfMeasure %}<br><small style="color:#94a3b8;">= {{ line.quantity|qty }} {{ line.baseUnitLabel }}</small>{% endif %}
                    </td>
                    <td style="text-align:right;"{% if idPrefix %} id="{{ idPrefix }}-price-{{ line.id }}"{% endif %}>{% if unpriced %}{{ line.displayUnitPrice is not null ? '$' ~ line.displayUnitPrice|price : 'TBD' }}{% else %}${{ line.displayUnitPrice|price }}{% endif %}</td>
                    <td style="text-align:right;"><strong>{% if unpriced %}{{ line.subtotal is not null ? '$' ~ line.subtotal|amount : 'TBD' }}{% else %}${{ line.subtotal|amount }}{% endif %}</strong></td>
                    <td>{{ perLineTaxLabel[loop.index0]|default('No tax') }}</td>
                    {# TBD rather than $0.00 while the line is unpriced — see #255. #}
                    <td style="text-align:right;">{% if unpriced %}{{ (perLineTax[loop.index0] ?? null) is not null ? '$' ~ perLineTax[loop.index0]|amount : 'TBD' }}{% else %}${{ perLineTax[loop.index0]|default(0)|amount }}{% endif %}</td>
                    {% if batch %}<td class="order-batch-cell">{{ line.batch|default('-') }}</td>{% endif %}
                </tr>
            {% else %}
                <tr><td colspan="{{ columns|length }}" class="empty-table-cell"><span>{{ emptyMessage }}</span></td></tr>
            {% endfor %}
            {# A main-line charge is a LINE ITEM by design, so it is a row of this table beside the
               products — exactly as each of these documents' own PDFs renders it. It used to be
               dumped into the invoice's footer with the other two placements, where it read as a
               total rather than as something billed. #}
            {% for feeLine in feeRows|default([]) %}
                <tr class="fee-line-row">
                    <td{% if nameClass %} class="{{ nameClass }}"{% endif %}><strong style="color:var(--admin-blue-hover);">{{ feeLine.label }}</strong></td>
                    <td>&mdash;</td>
                    <td>&mdash;</td>
                    <td>&mdash;</td>
                    <td style="text-align:center;">{% if showFeeQuantity|default(false) and feeLine.quantity != 1 %}{{ feeLine.quantity|qty }}{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}</td>
                    <td style="text-align:right;">&mdash;</td>
                    <td style="text-align:right;"><strong>${{ feeLine.amount|amount }}</strong></td>
                    <td>&mdash;</td>
                    <td style="text-align:right;">&mdash;</td>
                    {% if batch %}<td>&mdash;</td>{% endif %}
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
        {% if footTemplate is defined and footTemplate is not null %}
            {% include footTemplate with footContext|default({})|merge({columns: columns|length}) only %}
        {% endif %}
    </table>
</div>
